From 3276a885bb06af3c007277f9c2a1d14f8ae55ebf Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 11:23:45 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [daveey] fix(kanban): dispatch skips dedupe per episode, not
 per newest row; latest tracks the current reason

Hub 2026-11-12 11:07-11:12Z: t_01a1c75a008956d3 got 8 dispatch_skipped rows in 5 min while claim_deferred
flapped capacity <-> hub_cpu_at_or_above_limit tick to tick. record() compared only the newest row, so every
flip appended; at MAX_PER_EPISODE=16 recording stopped and latest() (quoted by the ready-wait notices) froze.

- record() dedupes against every skip of the episode: one row per distinct (reason, detail[, until]).
- The skip the latest tick saw lives in a machine-local, unsynced kanban_dispatch_skip_seen row per card
  (episode anchor kind@created_at, current payload, keys recorded, since, seen_at). latest() returns it, so the
  notice names the current reason after a flap or past the cap, unless a skip this host never saw (another
  machine's dispatcher, synced in) is newer than its last confirmation. Rows refresh at most once a minute
  while unchanged (a read-only precheck skips the write transaction), prune after 7 days / 5000 rows.
- The identical pair (two hub_cpu rows 28 s apart, on two cards at once): both cards are foreign pickup copies
  that kanban_worker_node evicts once the outbox drains and re-imports every tick (hub agent.log: "picked up 2
  card(s) ... ['t_01a1c75a008956d3', 't_01a1c9e64bd28e6d']" at 11:07:33; their event ids are renumbered
  contiguously). A re-import from a replica that has not pulled the host's own just-pushed skip back yet hid it
  from the next tick, which appended it again. Keys this host recorded in the episode now count, so it does
  not; the anchor uses kind+created_at because re-imports renumber event ids.

Tests: 40 alternating ticks stay at 2 rows with latest tracking each tick; latest past the cap; since kept and
writes throttled; re-imported copy missing its own skip; new episode; another writer's newer skip; two
connections recording the same key concurrently write one row (with and without the read-only precheck);
pruning.
